\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Chapter 5: Instrumental Variables Estimation of Single-Equation Linear Models
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
5.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Instrumental Variables and Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.1.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Motivation for Instrumental Variables Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To motivate the need for the method of instrumental variables, consider
 a linear population model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\beta_{K}x_{K}+u\\
E(u)=0,\ \mbox{Cov}(x_{j},u)=0,\ j=1,2,\ldots,K-1
\end{gather}

\end_inset

but where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 might be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, the explanatory variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

 are exogenous, but 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is potentially endogenous in equation (1).
 The endogeneity can come from an omitted variable, measurement error, etc.
 To fix ideas, it might help to think of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 as containing an omitted variable that is uncorrelated with all explanatory
 variables except 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we may be interested in a conditional expectation, we do not observe
 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

OLS esitmation of equation (1) generally results in inconsistent estimators
 of 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(x_{K},u)\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Further, without more information, we cannot consistently estimate any
 of the parameters in equation (1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The method of instrumental variables (IV) provides a general solution to
 the problem of an endogenous explanatory variable.
 To use the IV approach with 
\begin_inset Formula $k_{K}$
\end_inset

endogenous, we need an observable variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

, 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 in equation (1) that satisfies two conditions.
 First, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 must be uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},u)=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In other words, like 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous in equation (1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The second requirement involves the relationship between 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and the endogenous variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 A precise statemnt requires the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\emph on
all
\emph default
 the exogenous variables:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{K}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{1}+r_{k}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where, by definition of a linear projection error, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(r_{K})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}$
\end_inset

is uncorreated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The key assumption on this linear projection is that the coefficient on
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is nonzero:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\theta_{1}\neq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This condition is often loosely described as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 but that statement is not quite correct.
 The condition 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}\neq0$
\end_inset

 means that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
partially
\emph default
 correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 once the other exogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

 have been netted out.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is the only explanatory variable in equaiton (1), then the linear projection
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}=\delta_{0}+\theta_{1}z_{1}+r_{K}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}=\nicefrac{\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},x_{K})}{\mbox{Var}(z_{1})}$
\end_inset

, and so condition (5) and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},x_{K})\neq0$
\end_inset

 are the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

At this point, we should mention that we have put no restrictions on the
 distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 In many cases 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 will be both essentially continuous, but sometimes 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

, or both are discrete.
 In fact, one or both of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 can be binary variables, or have continuous and discrete characteristics
 at the same time.
 Equation (4) is simply a linear projections, and this is always defined
 when second moments of all variables are finite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 satisfies conditions (3) and (5), then it is said to be an 
\series bold
instrumental variable (IV)
\series default
 candidate for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 (Sometimes 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is simply called an 
\emph on
instrument
\emph default
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.) Because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

 are already uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, they serve as their own instrumental varibles in equation (1).
 In other words, the full list of instrumental variables is the same as
 the list of exogenous variables, but we often just refer to the instrument
 for the endogenous explanatory variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The linear projection in equation (4) is called a 
\series bold
reduced-form equation
\series default
 for the endogenous explanatory variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 In the context of single-equation linear models, a reduced form always
 involves writing an endogenous variable as a linear projection onto all
 exogenous variables.
 The 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

reduced form
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 terminology comes from simultanous equation analysis, and it makes more
 sens in that context.
 We use it in all IV contexts because it is a concise way of stating than
 an endogenous variable has been linearly projected onto the exogenous variables.
 The terminology also conveys there is nothing necessarily structural about
 equation (4).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the structural equation (1) and the reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, we obtain a reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 by plugging equation (4) into equation (1) and rearranging
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=\alpha_{0}+\alpha_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\alpha_{K}x_{K-1}+\lambda_{1}z_{1}+v
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v=u+\beta_{K}r_{K}$
\end_inset

 is the reduced-form error, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{j}=\beta_{j}+\beta_{K}\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}=\beta_{K}\theta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 By our assumptions, 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with all explanatory variables in equation (6), and so
 OLS consistently estimates the reduced-form parameters, the 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Estimates of the reduced-form parameters are sometimes of interest in their
 own right, but estimating the structural parameters is generally more useful.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can now show that the assumptions we have made on the IV 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 solve the 
\series bold
identification problem
\series default
 for the 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 in equation (1).
 By identification we mean that we can write 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 in terms of population moments in observable variables.
 To see how, write equation (1) as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the constant is abosrbed into 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=(1,x_{i2},\ldots,x_{iK})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

.
 Write the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of all exogenous variables as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}\equiv\left[\begin{array}{c}
\begin{array}{c}
1\\
x_{i2}\\
\vdots\\
x_{K-1}
\end{array}\\
z_{i1}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Assumptions (2) and (3) imply the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 population orthogonality conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}u_{i})=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Multiplying (7) through by 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, taking expectations, and using equation (8) gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\beta=E(z_{i}y_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}y_{i})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (9) represents a system of 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 linear equations in the 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 unknowns 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1},\ldots,\beta_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 This system has a unique solution if and only if the 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has full rank; that is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{rank}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K
\end{equation}

\end_inset

in which case the solution is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\beta=\left[E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}y_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The expectations 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}y_{i})$
\end_inset

 can be consistently estimated using a random sample on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y,z_{1})$
\end_inset

 and so equaiton (11) identifies the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is clear that condition (3) was used to obtain equation (11).
 But where have we used condition (5)? Let us maintain that there are no
 linear dependencies among the exogenous variables, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has full rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

; this simply rules out perfect collinearity in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the population.
 Then, it can be shown that equation (10) holds if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{1}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, along with the exogeneity condition (3), assumption (5) is the
 key identification condition.
 Assumption (10) is the 
\series bold
rank condition
\series default
 for identification.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given a random sample 
\begin_inset Formula $\left\{ (x_{i},y_{i},z_{i1}):i=1,\ldots,N\right\} $
\end_inset

 from the population, the 
\series bold
instrumental variables estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset


\series default
 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(N^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)=\left(Z^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}Z^{\prime}Y
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $Z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 are 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times K$
\end_inset

 data matrices and 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times1$
\end_inset

 data vector on the 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The consistency of this esitmator is immediate from equation (11) and the
 law of large numbers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When searching for instruments for an endogenous explanatory variable, condition
s (3) and (5) are equally importnat in identifying 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 There is, however, one practically important difference between them: condition
 (5) can be tested, whereas condition (3) must be maintained.
 The reason for this disparity is simple, the covariance in condition (3)
 involves 
\emph on
unobservable u
\emph default
, and therefore we cannot test anything about 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},u)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Testing condition (5) in the reduced form (4) is a simple matter of computing
 a 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 test after OLS estimation.
 Nothing guarantees the 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the equisite homoskedasticity assumption (Assumption OLS.3), so
 a heteroskedasiticity-robust 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 is often warranted.
 This statement is especially ture if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is a binary variable or some other variable with discrete characteristics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the conext of omitted variables, an instrumental variable, like a proxy
 variable, must be redundant in the structural model (that is, the model
 that explicitly contains the unobservables).
 However, unlike a proxy variable, an IV for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 should be 
\emph on
uncorrelated
\emph default
 with the omitted variable.
 Remember, we want a proxy variable to be highly correlated with the omitted
 variable.
 A proxy variables makes a poor IV, and an IV makes a poor proxy variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.1.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Multiple Instruments: Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider again the model (1) and (2), where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 can be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 Now, however, assume that we have more than one instrumental variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1},z_{2},\ldots,z_{M}$
\end_inset

 be variables such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{Cov}(z_{h},u)=0,\ h=1,2,\ldots,M
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so that each 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{h}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous in equation (1).
 If each of these has some partial correlation with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, we could have 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 different IV estimators.
 Actually, there are many more than this, more than we can count, since
 any linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K-1},z_{1},z_{2},\ldots,z_{M}$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 So which IV estimator should we use?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In Section 5.2.3 we show that, under certain assumptions, the 
\series bold
two-stage least squares (2SLS) estimator
\series default
 is the most efficient IV estimator.
 For now, we rely on intuition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To illustrate the method of 2SLS, define the vector of exogenous variables
 again by 
\begin_inset Formula $z\equiv(1,x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{K-1},z_{1},\ldots,z_{M})^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(L=K+M)$
\end_inset

.
 Out of all possible linear combinations of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 that can be used as an insturment for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, the method of 2SLS chooses that which is most highly correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 were exogenous, then this choice would imply that the best instrument for
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is simply itself.
 Ruling this case out, the linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 most highly correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is given by the lienar projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 Write the reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{K}=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{1}+\cdots+\theta_{M}z_{M}+r_{K}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where, by definition, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}$
\end_inset

 has zero mean and is uncorrelated with each right-hand-side variable.
 As any linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{K}^{*}\equiv\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{1}+\cdots+\delta_{K-1}x_{K-1}+\theta_{1}z_{1}+\cdots+\theta_{M}z_{M}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

In fact, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is often interpreted as the part of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 that is uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, it is because 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we coulde observe 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we would use it as an instrument for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 in equation (1) and use the IV estimator form the previous subsection.
 Since the 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{j}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}$
\end_inset

 are population parameters, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is not a usable instrument.
 However, as long as we make the standard assumption that there are no exact
 lienar dependencies among the exogenous variables, we can consistently
 esitmate the parameters in equation (13) by OLS.
 The sample analogues of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iK}^{*}$
\end_inset

 for each observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 are simply the OLS fitted values:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{iK}^{*}=\hat{\delta}_{0}+\hat{\delta}_{1}x_{i1}+\cdots+\hat{\delta}_{K-1}x_{i,K-1}+\hat{\theta}_{1}z_{i1}+\cdots+\hat{\theta}_{M}z_{iM}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, for each observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, define the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}\equiv(1,x_{i1},\ldots,x_{i,K-1},\hat{x}_{iK})^{\prime},\ i=1,\ldots,N$
\end_inset

.
 Using 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 as the instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 gives the IV estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}y_{i}\right)=\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}X\right)^{-1}\hat{X}^{\prime}Y
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where unity is also the first element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The IV estimator in equation (16) turns out to be an OLS estimator.
 To see this fact, note that the 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times(K+1)$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}$
\end_inset

 can be expressed as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}=Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}X=P_{Z}X$
\end_inset

, where the projection matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{Z}=Z(Z^{\prime}Z)^{-1}Z^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 is idempotent and symmetric.
 Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{X}^{\prime}X=X^{\prime}P_{Z}X=\left(P_{Z}X\right)^{\prime}P_{Z}X=\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}$
\end_inset

.
 Plugging this expression into equation (16) shows that the IV estimator
 that uses instruments 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}=\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}\hat{X}^{\prime}Y$
\end_inset

.
 The name 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

two-stage least squares
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 comes from this procedure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To summarize, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 can be obtianed from the following steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Obtain fitted values 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{K}$
\end_inset

 from the regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
x_{K}\ \mbox{on}\ 1,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},z_{1},\ldots,z_{M}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 subscript is omitted for simplicity.
 This is called the 
\series bold
first-stage regression
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run the OLS regression
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y\ \mbox{on}\ 1,x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1},\hat{x}_{K}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This is called the 
\series bold
second-stage regression
\series default
, and it produces the 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 2SLS esitmator and the IV estimator from Section 5.1.1 are identical when
 there is only one isntrument for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

.
 Unless stated otherwise, we mean 2SLS whenever we talk about IV estimation
 of a single equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

What is the analogue of the condition (5) when more than one instrument
 is available? 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has full column rank if and only if at least one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}$
\end_inset

 in equation (13) is nonzero.
 The intuition behind this requirement is pretty clear: we need at least
 one exogenous variable that does not appear in equation (1) to induce varaition
 in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 that cannot be explained by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1},\ldots,x_{K-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Identification of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 depend on the values of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{h}$
\end_inset

 in equation (13).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Testing the rank condition with a single endogenous explanatory variable
 and multiple instruments is straightforward.
 In equation (13) we simply test the null hypothesis
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
H_{0}:\theta_{1}=0,\theta_{2}=0,\ldots,\theta_{M}=0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

against the alternative that at least one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is different from zero.
 This test gives a compelling reason for explicity running the first-stage
 regression.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}$
\end_inset

 in equation (13) satisfies the OLS homoskedasticity assumption OLS.3, a
 standard 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

 statistic or Lagrange multiplier statistic can be used to test hypothesis
 (19).
 Often a heteroskedasticity-robust statistic is more appropriate, especially
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

 has discrete characteristics.
 If we cannot reject hypothesis (19) against the alternative that at least
 one 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{h}$
\end_inset

 is different from zero, at a reasonably small significance level, then
 we should have serious reservations about the proposed 2SLS procedure:
 the instruments do not pass a minimal requirement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The model with a single endogenous variable is said to be 
\series bold
overidentified
\series default
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $M>1$
\end_inset

 and there are 
\begin_inset Formula $M-1$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
overidentifing restrictions
\series default
.
 This terminology comes from the fact that, if each 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{h}$
\end_inset

 has some partial correlation with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}$
\end_inset

, then we have 
\begin_inset Formula $M-1$
\end_inset

 more exogenous variables than needed to identify the parameters in equation
 (1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
5.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

General Treatment of Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consistency
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We now summarize the asymptotic results for 2SLS in a single-equation model
 with perhaps several endogenous variables among the explanatory variables.
 Write the population model as in equation (7), where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

 and generally includes unity.
 Several elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 may be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 As usual, we assume that a random sample is available from the population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\emph on
2SLS.1:
\series default
 
\emph default
For some 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we do not specify where the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 come from, but any exogenous elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, including a constant, are included in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Unless every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 will have to contain variables obtained from outside the model.
 The zero conditional mean assumption, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, implies Assumption 2SLS.1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The next assumption contains the general rank condition for single-equation
 analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
2SLS.2:
\series default
 
\emph on
(a) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=L$
\end_inset

; (b) 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Technically, part (a) of this assumption is needed, but it is not especially
 important, since the exogenous variables, unless chosen unwisely, will
 be linearly independent in the population (as well as in a typical sample).
 Part (b) is the crucial 
\series bold
rank condition
\series default
 for identificaiton.
 In a precise sense it means that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is sufficiently linearly related to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 so that rank 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has full column rank.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, so that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}=x_{i}$
\end_inset

, Assumption 2SLS.1 reduces to Assumption OLS.1 and Assumption 2SLS.2 reduced
 to Assumption OLS.2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Necessary for the rank condition is the 
\series bold
order condition
\series default
, 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

.
 In other words, we must have at least as many instruments as we have explanator
y variables.
 If we do not have as many instruments as right-hand-side variables, then
 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is not identified.
 However, 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq K$
\end_inset

 is no guarantee that 2SLS.2b holds: the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 might not be appropriately correlated with the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We already know how to test Assumption 2SLS.2b with a single endogenous explanato
ry variable.
 In the general case, it is possible to test Assumption 2SLS.2b, given a
 random sample on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,z)$
\end_inset

, essentially be performing tests on the sample analogue of 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{Z^{\prime}X}{N}$
\end_inset

.
 The tests are somewhat complicated.
 Often we estimate the reduced form for each engoenous explanatory variable
 to make sure that at least one element of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 not in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is significant.
 This is not sufficient for the rank condition in general, but it can help
 us determine if the rank condition fails.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Using linear projections, there is a simple way to see how Assumptions 2SLS.1
 and 2SLS.2 identify 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 First, assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular, we can always write the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Pi$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi=\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 Since each column of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 can be consistently esitmated by regressing the appropriate element of
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, for the purposes of identification of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, we can treat 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 as known.
 Write 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, the 2SLS estimator is effectively the IV estimator using instruments
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Multiplying equation (7) by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, taking expectations, and rearranging gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})\beta=E(x_{i}^{*}y_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified by 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta=\left[E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(x_{i}^{*}y_{i})$
\end_inset

 provided 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is nonsingular.
 But
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})=\Pi^{\prime}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})
\]

\end_inset

and this matrix is nonsingular if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 has rank 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

; that is, if and only if Assumption 2SLS.2b holds.
 If 2SLS.2b fails, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is singular and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is not identified.
 (Note that, because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})$
\end_inset

.
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is identified if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})=K$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 2SLS estimator can be written as in equation (16) or as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\beta}=\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}y_{i}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We have the following consistency result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
5.1 (Consistency of 2SLS):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions 2SLS.1 and 2SLS.2, the 2SLS estimator obtianed from a random
 sample is consistent for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=\beta+\left[\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}\right)\right]^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}x_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}z_{i}u_{i}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and, using Assumptions 2SLS.1 and 2SLS.2, apply the law of large numbers to
 each term along with Slutsky's theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Asymptotic Normality of Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The asymptotic normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)$
\end_inset

 follows from the asymptotic normality of 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{N}z_{i}u_{i}$
\end_inset

, which follows from the central limit theorm under Assumption 2SLS.1 and
 mild finite second-moment assumptions.
 The asymptotic variance is simplest under a homoskedasticity assumption:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Assumption*

\series bold
\shape up
2SLS.3:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}^{2}z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=\sigma^{2}E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}=E(u_{i}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This assumption is the same as Assumption OLS.3 except that the vector of
 instruments appears in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 By the usual LIE argument, sufficient for Assumption 2SLS.3 is the assumption
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E(u_{i}^{2}|z_{i})=\sigma^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which is the same as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|z_{i})=\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $E(u_{i}|z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 (When 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 contains endogenous elements, it makes no sense to make assumptions about
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
5.2 (Asymptotic Normality of 2SLS):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)$
\end_inset

 is asymptotically normally distributed with mean zero and variance matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sigma^{2}\left(\left[E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}\left[E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]\right)^{-1}=\sigma^{2}\left[E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})\right]^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Pi$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $1\times K$
\end_inset

 vector of linear projections.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The right-hand side of equation (23) is convenient because it has the same
 form as the expression for the OLS estimator, but with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 replaced 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, we can easily obtain a simple expression for the asymptotic
 varaince for a single coefficient: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}\ \sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}_{K}-\beta_{K})=\nicefrac{\sigma^{2}}{\mbox{Var}(r_{K}^{*})}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the population residual from regressing 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{K}^{*}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{*},\ldots,x_{K-1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 (where, typically, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{*}=1$
\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The matrix in expression (23) is easily estimated using sample averages.
 To estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 we will need appropriate estimates of the 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Define the 
\series bold
2SLS residuals
\series default
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{u}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta},\ i=1,\ldots,N
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Note carefully that these residuals are 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 the residuals from the second-stage OLS regression that can be used to
 obtain the 2SLS estimates.
 The residuals from the second-stage regression are 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

.
 Any 2SLS software routine will compute equation (24) as the 2SLS residuals,
 and these are what we need to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Given the 2SLS residuals, a consistent (though not unbiased) estimator of
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}\equiv(N-K)^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Many regression packages use the degrees-of-freedom adjustment 
\begin_inset Formula $N-K$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, but this usage does not affec tthe consistency of the estimator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{x}_{i}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)^{-1}=\hat{\sigma}^{2}\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is a valid estimator of the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 under Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3.
 The (asymptotic) standard error of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{j}$
\end_inset

 is just the square root of the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th diagonal element of matrix (26).
 Asymptotic confidence intervals and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistics are obtained in the usual fashion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.3.
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Asymptotic Efficiency of Two-stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The appeal of 2SLS comes from its efficiency in a class of IV estimators.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
5.3 (Relative Efficiency of 2SLS):
\series default
 
\emph on
Under Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3, the 2SLS estimator is efficient in the class
 of all instrumental variables estimators using instruments linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the 2SLS estimator, and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be any other IV estimator using instruments linear in 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Let the instruments for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Gamma$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 is an 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times K$
\end_inset

 nonstochastic matrix.
 (Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 random vector in the population.) We assume that the rank condition holds
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For 2SLS, the choice of IVs is effectively 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*\prime}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Pi$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi=\left[E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\equiv D^{-1}C$
\end_inset

.
 (In both cases, we can replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent estimators without changing the asymptotic variances.) Now, under
 Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3, we know the asymptotic variance of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}\left[E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*^{\prime}}=z_{i}^{\prime}\Pi$
\end_inset

.
 It is straightforward to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\beta}-\beta)\right]=\sigma^{2}\left[E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}\left[E(\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\right]\left[E(x_{i}\tilde{x}_{i})\right]^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 To show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}(\tilde{\beta}-\beta)\right]-\mbox{AVar}\left[\sqrt{N}(\hat{\beta}-\beta)\right]$
\end_inset

 is positive semidefinite (p.s.d.), it suffices to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})-E(x_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\left[E(\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 is p.s.d.
 But 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{\prime}=x_{i}^{*\prime}+r_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

, and so 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\tilde{x}_{i}r_{i}^{\prime})=0$
\end_inset

.
 It follows that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{*\prime})$
\end_inset

, and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})-E(x_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\left[E(\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=E(x_{i}^{*}x_{i}^{*\prime})-E(x_{i}^{*}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\left[E(\tilde{x}_{i}\tilde{x}_{i}^{\prime})\right]^{-1}E(\tilde{x}_{i}x_{i}^{*\prime})=E(s_{i}^{*}s_{i}^{*\prime})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}^{*}=x_{i}^{*}-L(x_{i}^{*}|\tilde{x}_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the population residual from the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $E(s_{i}^{*}s_{i}^{*\prime})$
\end_inset

 is p.s.d., the proof is complete.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Theorem 5.3 is vacuous when 
\begin_inset Formula $L=K$
\end_inset

 because any (nonsingular) choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Gamma$
\end_inset

 leads to the same estimator: the IV estimator derived in Secion 5.1.1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, Theorem 5.3 implies that, under Assumptions 2SLS.1-2SLS.3, the
 OLS estimator is efficient in the class of all estimators using instruments
 in all exogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Why? Because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and so 
\begin_inset Formula $L(x_{i}|z_{i})=x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another important implication of Theorem 5.3 is that, asymptotically, we
 always do better by using as many instruments as are available, at least
 under homoskedasticity.
 This conclusion follows because using a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 as instruments corresponds to using a particular linear combination of
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For certain subsets we might achieve the same efficiency as 2SLS using
 all of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, but we can do no better.
 This observation makes it tempting to add many instruments so that 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is much larger than 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

.
 Unfortunately, 2SLS estimators based on many overidentifying restrictions
 can cause finite sample problems.
 Because Assumption 2SLS.3 is assumed for Theorem 5.3, it is not surprising
 that more efficient estimators are available if Assumption 2SLS.3 fails.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hypothesis Testing with Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have already seen that tsting hypotheses about a single 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is straightforward using an asymptotic 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic, which has an asymptotic normal distribution under the null;
 some prefer to use the 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 distribution when 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is small.
 Generally, one should be aware that the normal and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 approximations can be poor if 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is small.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To test multiple linear restrictions of the form 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:R\beta=r$
\end_inset

, the Wald statistic is the same as for traditional OLS regression, but
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{V}$
\end_inset

 given by equation (26).
 The Wald statistic, as usual, is a limiting null 
\begin_inset Formula $\chi_{Q}^{2}$
\end_inset

 distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.5
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Heteroskedasticity-Robust Inference for Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Assumption 2SLS.3 can be restrictive, so we should have a variance matrix
 estimator that is robust in the presense of heteroskedasticity of unknown
 form.
 As usual, we need to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 along with 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 Under Assumptions 2SLS.1 and 2SLS.2 only, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{AVar}(\hat{\beta})$
\end_inset

 can be estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\hat{u}_{i}^{2}\hat{x}_{i}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Sometimes this matrix is multiplied by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{N}{(N-K)}$
\end_inset

 as a degrees-of-freedom adjustment.
 This heteroskedasticity-robust estimator can be used anywhere the estimator
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}^{2}(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X})^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 In particular, the square roots of the diagonal elements of the matrix
 (27) are the heteroskedasticiy-robust standard errors for 2SLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
5.2.6
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Potential Pitfalls with Two-Stage Least Squares
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When properly applied, the method of instrumental variables can be a powerful
 tool for estimating structural equations using nonexperimental data.
 Nevertheless, there are some problems that one can encounter when applying
 IV in practice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

One thing to remember is that, unlike OLS under a zero conditional mean
 assumption, IV methods are never unbiased when at least one explanatory
 variable is endogenous in the model.
 In fact, under standard distributional assumptions, the expected value
 of the 2SLS estimator does not even exist.
 In the case when all endogenous variables have homoskedastic normal distributio
ns with expectations linear in the exogenous variables, the number of moments
 of the 2SLS estimator that exists is one fewer than the number of overidentifyi
ng restrictions.
 This finding implies that when the number of instruments equals the number
 of explanatory variables, the IV estimator does not have an expected value.
 This is one reason we rely on large-sample analysis to justify 2SLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Even in large samples, IV methods can be ill-behaved if we have 
\series bold
weak instruments
\series default
.
 Consider the simple model 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{1}+u$
\end_inset

, where we use 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 as an instrument for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},x_{1})\neq0$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 of the IV estimator is easily shown to be
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}_{1}=\beta_{1}+\frac{\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},u)}{\mbox{Cov}(z_{1},x_{1})}
\]

\end_inset

which can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}\ \hat{\beta}_{1}=\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{x_{1}}}\right)\left[\frac{\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},u)}{\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},x_{1})}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Corr}(\cdot,\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denotes correlation.
 From this equation we see that if 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 are correlated, the inconsistency in the IV estimator gets arbitrarily
 large as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},x_{1})$
\end_inset

 gets close to zero.
 Thus, seemingly small correlations between 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 can cause severe inconsistency, and therefore severe finite sample bias,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is only weakly correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 In fact, it may be better to just use OLS, even if we look only at the
 inconsistencies in the estimators.
 To see why, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{1}$
\end_inset

 denote the OLS estimator and write its 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{p\lim}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{p\lim}(\tilde{\beta}_{1})=\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{u}}{\sigma_{x_{1}}}\right)\mbox{Corr}(x_{1},u)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Comparing equations (28) and (29), the signs of the inconsistency of OLS
 and IV can be different.
 Further, the magnitude of the inconsistency in OLS is smaller than that
 of the IV estimator if 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mbox{Corr}(x_{1},u)\right|\cdot\left|\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},x_{1})\right|<\left|\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},u)\right|$
\end_inset

.
 This simple inequality makes it apparent that a weak instrument, captured
 here be small 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},x_{1})\right|$
\end_inset

, can easily make IV have more asymptotic bias than OLS.
 Unfortunately, we never observe 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, so we cannot know how large 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mbox{Corr}(z_{1},u)\right|$
\end_inset

 is relative to 
\begin_inset Formula $\left|\mbox{Corr}(x_{1},u)\right|$
\end_inset

.
 But what is certain is that a small correlation between 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

, which we can estimate should raise concerns, even if we think 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

almost
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Another potential problem with applying 2SLS and other IV procedures is
 that the 2SLS standard errors have a tendency to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

large.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 What is typically meant by this statement is either that 2SLS coefficients
 are statistically insignifcant or that the 2SLS standard errors are much
 large than the OLS standard errors.
 Not surprisingly, the magnitudes of the 2SLS standard errors, depend among
 other things, on the quality of the instruments used in estimation.
\end_layout

